\section{File layout}{

Each level is split into tiles of at most \code{tile_size} rows, and each tile
is one Parquet row group. Levels follow each other in ascending LOD
order, coarsest first. A tile is a run of consecutive cell ids: A5 ids
follow a space-filling curve, so such a run is a compact patch of the
globe. A level is split down the A5 index hierarchy only where a run
holds more than \code{tile_size} cells, which makes tiles coarse where data
is sparse and fine where it is dense, and keeps every level sorted by
cell id.

The file's key-value metadata holds a JSON index under \code{"a5pyramid"}:
the leaves' resolution, the value columns with their types and reducers,
each level's LOD, row count and row groups, and the bounding box of each
row group, computed from its cells' boundaries. A reader fetches the
footer, picks the level for the zoom and the row groups whose boxes meet
the view, and decodes only those. \code{"a5_index"} records the A5 index
version of the ids, \code{"1.0"}, as across the a5R stack.

The columns are \code{cell} (\code{uint64}), \code{lod} (\code{uint8}), \code{ncell} (\code{uint32})
and the value columns with their types, \code{NA} stored as null. \code{cell} and
\code{ncell} are delta encoded and \code{lod} dictionary encoded; Parquet
statistics are kept for \code{cell} and \code{lod} only, so engines such as DuckDB
can skip row groups by LOD or cell while the footer stays small. The
format is specified in \code{FORMAT.md} in the package source
(\code{src/rust/a5pyramid/FORMAT.md}), with a reading recipe for hyparquet.
